---
title: The first one they killed
description: Priscillian of Ávila is called the first heretic executed in Christian history. The charge on paper wasn't heresy — it was sorcery. The gap between those two things is the whole story, and it's the moment the church learned it could borrow the sword.
date: May 2026
tags: [history]
---

You can find him in any reference work with the same caption: Priscillian of &Aacute;vila, the first heretic in Christian history to be executed for his beliefs. Beheaded at Trier, sometime around 385, along with a handful of his followers. Roughly seventy years after the persecutions ended &mdash; after the empire stopped killing Christians &mdash; Christians worked out how to start killing each other. That&rsquo;s the line. It&rsquo;s true enough to keep repeating.

But the caption hides the thing that actually matters, which is that he was not, on paper, executed for heresy. He was executed for *magic*.

Hold onto that gap. The whole story lives in it.

:::divider

Start with the man, because the man is not what the word &ldquo;heretic&rdquo; trains you to picture.

Priscillian was a wealthy, classically educated layman from Roman Gallaecia &mdash; the far northwest corner of Iberia, up where Spain runs out into the Atlantic. Sometime in the 370s he started teaching, and what he taught was not some lurid secret cosmology. It was *rigor*. Fasting. Celibacy. Abstinence from meat and wine. Long immersion in scripture, including the books the church was still arguing about. A Christianity stripped down to intensity &mdash; the spiritual life as continual, demanding contact with God, available to anyone willing to actually do it.

And people came. That was the problem.

They came in numbers, and they came across the usual lines. His movement welcomed women as full participants &mdash; not as an audience but as equals, organized alongside the men into the committed and the abstinent. Wealthy laypeople took it up. It spread fast enough, and sideways enough, that the bishops who were *supposed* to be the channel for that kind of fervor found it running outside them. A lay teacher with a charismatic following that crosses class and gender lines and doesn&rsquo;t route through the institution &mdash; that is a threat the institution recognizes in its body before it can name it.

So his supporters made him a bishop. Bishop of &Aacute;vila, around 380, partly in defiance, which only raised the stakes. Now the irregular movement had a mitre.

:::divider

The machine that came for him is depressingly familiar.

:::timeline
- when: 380
  who: Council of Saragossa
  body: Hydatius of M&eacute;rida and Ithacius of Ossonoba bring the charges &mdash; a moving target, which is itself a tell. Manichaeism. Gnostic dualism. Sorcery. The accusation shifted depending on the room, because the accusation was never really the point. The point was that Priscillian had to be stopped, and the charges were tools for stopping him.

- when: ~381&ndash;383
  who: Appeals to Rome and Milan
  body: There were appeals &mdash; to Pope Damasus in Rome, to Ambrose in Milan, to officials at court &mdash; and for a while the appeals worked, more or less.

- when: 383
  who: Magnus Maximus seizes the western throne
  body: The politics changed underneath everyone. A usurper needs legitimacy the way a drowning man needs air. Proving yourself a defender of orthodox Christianity is cheap legitimacy, and it was lying right there.

- when: 384
  who: Synod at Bordeaux
  body: Condemned at a synod in Bordeaux, Priscillian appealed *over* the bishops&rsquo; heads to the emperor himself, at Trier &mdash; gambling that imperial justice would be cleaner than ecclesiastical justice. It was the worst bet of his life.

- when: ~385
  who: Trial and execution at Trier
  active: true
  body: He was handed to the praetorian prefect, Evodius, tried, tortured, and convicted. Not of false doctrine. Roman law had no death penalty for being wrong about the Trinity. So they convicted him of *maleficium* &mdash; sorcery, black magic &mdash; with a side charge of immorality. Crimes the state could actually kill you for. Priscillian and several companions were beheaded at Trier.
:::end

:::divider

The textbook calls him the first heretic executed. The court called him a sorcerer. Both are right. And I keep thinking about the distance between them &mdash; the invention being made in that room. Everyone involved understood the trial was about doctrine. But heresy wasn&rsquo;t a capital crime. So they dressed the religious offense in a criminal one, and the secular arm obliged.

:::thesis
How to make the church&rsquo;s enemy into the empire&rsquo;s criminal, so the empire would do the killing the church couldn&rsquo;t.
Henry Chadwick called Priscillian the first and, in all of antiquity, very nearly the *only* heretic to suffer formal capital punishment from the state. The grim achievement wasn&rsquo;t just a death. It was the discovery of a *method*.
:::end

:::callout
**Chadwick&rsquo;s judgment:** The major modern study &mdash; *Priscillian of Avila* (1976) &mdash; establishes this as a turning point: the first time the machinery of secular prosecution was successfully borrowed to settle a doctrinal dispute. That method had a long future. You can draw a straight line from this trial to everything that learned to use it.
:::end

:::divider

Here is the detail that keeps me up, though. The people who should have cheered didn&rsquo;t. The best figures in the church at that exact moment recoiled in real time &mdash; not centuries later with the benefit of hindsight, but immediately, while it was happening.

:::hinge
- tag: The accuser
  title: Ithacius of Ossonoba
  body: The engine of the prosecution. Pushed the case from council to court to emperor, shifting charges as needed. The man who did the work of making a bishop into a criminal.

- tag: The dissenter
  title: Martin of Tours
  body: Went to Trier specifically to stop it. Pleaded with Maximus for the men&rsquo;s lives. When the executions went ahead anyway, he was so disgusted he refused communion with Ithacius and the bishops who had pushed the case. According to Sulpicius Severus, he was tormented for the rest of his life by even the brief, pressured contact he&rsquo;d been forced into with them.
:::end

Ambrose condemned it. Pope Siricius condemned it. Their objection wasn&rsquo;t that Priscillian was secretly orthodox &mdash; most of them probably assumed he wasn&rsquo;t. Their objection was that *this is not how the church is allowed to win.* You do not hand a brother to the executioner over a doctrine. The instant the thing was done, the conscience of the church flinched.

And it cost the accusers. The backlash was real; Ithacius was eventually disgraced and deposed, and when Maximus himself fell a few years later, the verdict curdled into scandal. Priscillian&rsquo;s followers carried his body home and treated him as a martyr. The first man the church killed for heresy was promptly venerated as a saint by the people who loved him.

:::divider

For fifteen hundred years, all we had was the prosecution&rsquo;s file. Everything we knew about what Priscillian *believed* came from the men who wanted him dead, or from later writers repeating them. His own writings had been hunted down and burned. The heretic doesn&rsquo;t get to testify; that&rsquo;s part of what being declared a heretic means.

Then, in 1885, a scholar named Georg Schepss was working in the university library at W&uuml;rzburg and found eleven tractates &mdash; treatises in Priscillian&rsquo;s own hand, or close to it, preserved in a manuscript old enough to be nearly contemporary. The accused, after a millennium and a half of silence, finally got to speak in his own voice.

And the voice doesn&rsquo;t match the file.

What&rsquo;s in the tractates is an ascetic and a Bible-teacher: defenses of strict personal piety, careful exegesis of Paul, arguments *against* the very Manichaean dualism he&rsquo;d been condemned for. The lurid Gnostic cosmology, the evil-matter doctrine, the secret heresies &mdash; they&rsquo;re elusive in his actual words, when they appear at all. The scholars who&rsquo;ve worked the texts most closely, Chadwick and Virginia Burrus among them, conclude that the tractates disrupt the whole inherited story. The monster on the page and the monster in the chronicles do not line up.

:::callout
**Not everyone agrees.** The Catholic tradition has held that the smoke implies fire &mdash; that where there are charges of Manichaeism and dualism repeated across multiple sources and centuries, something heterodox was likely present. But the documents made it permanently harder to be sure the men who killed him were even *right*, let alone justified.
:::end

He may not have been a heretic at all. He may simply have been ungovernable, and devout, and in the way.

:::divider

Two more things, because they&rsquo;re too strange to leave out.

:::callout-accent
The earliest clear appearance of the *Comma Johanneum* &mdash; that fabricated Trinitarian clause in First John, the one that wasn&rsquo;t in any Greek manuscript for a thousand years &mdash; shows up in the Latin of Priscillian&rsquo;s own *Liber Apologeticus*. Raymond Brown traced it there. So the first man executed as a heretic is also, possibly, the pen under which Christianity&rsquo;s most famous textual forgery first surfaces. The tradition killed him for corrupting the faith, and then spent the next twelve centuries quietly canonizing a verse that may have started with him. I don&rsquo;t know what to do with that except sit in it.
:::end

:::callout-accent
There&rsquo;s a long-running scholarly suggestion, Chadwick again among its proponents, that the bones venerated for over a thousand years at Santiago de Compostela &mdash; the relics of the apostle James, one of the great pilgrimage destinations of the Christian world &mdash; may in fact be Priscillian&rsquo;s, carried home by his devoted followers and eventually mistaken for something safer to love. It can&rsquo;t be proven. But picture it: the first heretic the church ever put to death, lying in honor at the end of a holy road, with millions of pilgrims kneeling at the tomb of the man their institution killed.
:::end

:::divider

I keep circling the same thing with all of this. People expect the history of a religion to be a story of a pure thing getting protected. What it actually looks like, up close, is a living thing arguing with itself &mdash; and the argument includes its own conscience.

Because that&rsquo;s the part the caption leaves out. It wasn&rsquo;t only Ithacius in that story. It was also Martin of Tours, refusing communion with the executioners while the bodies were still warm, certain in his gut that a line had been crossed that the gospel did not permit. The same tradition produced the man who did the killing and the man who could not stomach it, and it kept *both* of them &mdash; kept Martin as a saint, and kept, eventually, the buried memory of Priscillian as something other than a villain, surfacing again the moment his own words were found. A tradition that only protected itself would have let the file stand. This one kept leaking the truth back out: in Martin&rsquo;s revulsion, in the backlash that broke Ithacius, in a German library in 1885, in the persistent rumor at the end of the Camino.

If the Logos is a living principle moving through history rather than a fortress guarding a frozen deposit, this is exactly its signature &mdash; not the purity, but the self-correction. The dissent that won&rsquo;t die. The buried voice that comes back. The text, and the conscience behind the text, refusing to stay closed.

They killed the first one in 385 and called it defending the faith. The better people in the room knew right then it was something else. Fifteen centuries later we dug up his actual words, and it turned out the dissenters in the room had been right to flinch.

I&rsquo;ve been on the road now for a while. And I keep finding the same thing in every century I dig into: the tradition arguing with itself. The institution doing what institutions do &mdash; protecting the structure at the expense of the people. And somewhere in the same room, a dissenter who can&rsquo;t stomach it. Martin at Trier. Ambrose writing letters. The conscience of the church, flinching in real time. That&rsquo;s the part I hold onto. Not the purity. The self-correction.

:::thesis
The tradition has always been arguing with itself. The arguing is not the corruption of it. The arguing might be the most alive thing in it.
:::end

:::colophon
<span class="sig">Sources:</span> Henry Chadwick, *Priscillian of Avila* (1976) &mdash; the major modern study. Sulpicius Severus, *Life of Martin* &mdash; contemporary account of Martin&rsquo;s intervention at Trier. Georg Schepss, discoverer of the W&uuml;rzburg tractates (1885). Virginia Burrus, *The Making of a Heretic* &mdash; gender, authority, and the construction of Priscillian&rsquo;s deviance.
:::end